in @prism
in @meta
in @glass
in @magic
in @magic/contract
in @frame
in @epistemologic
in @epistemologic/cybernetic
in @epistemologic/cybernetic/bateson_learning

# @magic/frame — the cognitive-altitude adapter species. Bridges the
# @magic family (form/process at substrate altitude, #80) with the
# @frame family (cognitive-order architecture, #82) honestly via the
# adapter pattern, NOT decorative inheritance.
#
# Tick after frame.mirror landing (#82, tick 23) and after the
# @magic/distinction adapter precedent (tick 15). This species lands
# the cross-family composition route that the canonical spec for #82
# (§1 constraint-2) forward-promised to close Seam C1: the "@frame IS
# @magic at cognitive altitude" claim was load-bearing in name but
# hedged in body; this adapter makes the structural identity
# empirically discharged via explicit carrier flow plus a bilateral
# predicate consumers can name in `requires` clauses.
#
# Spec: `docs/specs/adapter-magic-frame-spec.md` (Mara's @magic ↔
# @frame adapter spec, 2026-06-19 late morning, commissioned by Alex
# via Reed). This shard IS the substrate-decl that the spec
# forward-promised.
#
# CRQ for this tick: how to discharge the "@frame IS @magic at
# cognitive altitude" structural identity claim mechanically WITHOUT
# the decorative inheritance Seam caught at tick 7-10 (constraint C3)
# and the canonical-spec hedge preserved at #82's family-root?
#
# Answer: the same adapter pattern @magic/distinction demonstrated at
# tick 15. Take @frame carriers; MAP them to @magic carriers; declare
# a bilateral predicate that discharges BOTH family verdicts in one
# clause. The structural inheritance lives in the explicit map plus
# the doubled bilateral, not in an `in` clause that buys namespace
# ancestry without carrier flow.
#
# === The adapter pattern (per spec §2) ===
#
# @frame's family-root carriers (per `shards/frame.mirror` tick 23
# Seam consolidation):
#   - `frame`         the gauge-visible structure of the operator's
#                     cognitive position
#   - `operator`      the typed locus the cognitive_order measures
#   - `morphism`      the action whose commutator with D_F is probed
#   - `commutator`    the bracketed `[D_F, a]` pair Tomm-probe returns
#   - `perturbation`  what bounded_commutator_check applies
#
# @magic's family-root carriers (per `shards/magic.mirror` #80):
#   - `magic_surface`    the 5-op gauge interface observable from
#                        outside the encapsulation
#   - `magic_mechanism`  the matter-hidden trick (encapsulated content)
#   - `magic_invariant`  the named promise the surface commits to
#                        delivering via the mechanism
#   - `magic_contract`   the record `{surface, mechanism, promise}`
#
# The substrate-pull-correct mapping (per spec §2.2):
#
#   frame             ↔ magic_surface
#   operator-state    ↔ magic_mechanism
#   operator-intent   ↔ magic_invariant
#
# At the substrate altitude: frame and magic_surface are both content-
# addressed typed refs with byte-equality identity; the lift IS the
# typed reframe at the @magic altitude. The cognitive state at the
# operator IS the matter-hidden trick at cognitive altitude
# (encapsulated by the substrate's parametric carriers). The operator's
# intent IS what they commit to delivering via the frame they present.
#
# === The doubled-bilateral structural shape ===
#
# @magic/distinction (tick 15) discharges ONE parent-family predicate
# (Spencer-Brown's `distinction_well_formed`). @magic/frame discharges
# TWO predicates (one parent-family, one child-family) in one clause:
#
#   - `invariant_preserved` (@magic/contract, per `shards/magic/
#     contract.mirror`): the contract's promise field IS what the
#     boundary harness audits at @io per recognition #57.
#   - `bounded_commutator_check` (@frame family-root): the substrate-
#     architectural verifier of frame-flexibility per recognition #82
#     and #37 (Pask agreement).
#
# The doubled load is structural: the "IS" claim at cognitive altitude
# requires both families' bilaterals to hold simultaneously. A consumer
# that names `requires frame_satisfies_magic(c, op, p)` declares
# bilateral commitment that BOTH the @magic invariant preservation
# AND the @frame bounded-commutator check discharge under the lift.
#
# This is the FIRST cross-family bilateral predicate in @magic that
# carries two family verdicts simultaneously. The pattern recurs;
# future adapters (@magic/kintsugi, @magic/autopoiesis, @magic/
# spectral_uuid named in spec §4) will follow the same shape.
#
# === Why `frame_satisfies_magic` takes operator + perturbation
#     as explicit parameters ===
#
# Per `shards/frame.mirror` line 335: `bounded_commutator_check(
# operator: operator, f: frame, perturbation: perturbation) -> verdict`.
# The @frame-family predicate needs an explicit operator and explicit
# perturbation; neither lives in `magic_contract`'s three fields
# (surface, mechanism, promise).
#
# The substrate-pull-correct signature, per Reed's brief and per
# `[[feedback-no-bare-types]]`, takes operator and perturbation as
# typed parameters rather than attempting to extract them from the
# contract's encapsulated surface. The contract carries the
# @magic-altitude shape; the @frame-altitude shape needs its own
# typed inputs. Consumer species name all three at the `requires`
# clause boundary.
#
# === The inverse `frame_from_surface` is type-relabeling ===
#
# Per spec §2.3 and §8.4: the inverse exists because both `frame` and
# `magic_surface` are typed refs with byte-equality identity; the lift
# is type-relabeling, not data transformation. Round-trip identity
# (`frame_as_surface(frame_from_surface(s)) = s` for compatible
# surfaces) is the cheapest falsification check.
#
# This is the FOURTH lifting action, one more than @magic/distinction's
# three. The cognitive-altitude adapter needs the inverse so the
# bilateral's `bounded_commutator_check` clause can name the @frame
# instance underlying the contract's surface.
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
#   adapter pattern (GoF)       Gamma-Helm-Johnson-Vlissides 1994
#                               (*Design Patterns*, Structural Patterns:
#                               Adapter). OO-canonical. Inherited via
#                               @magic/distinction tick 15.
#   functor adapter             Mac Lane 1971 (*Categories for the
#                               Working Mathematician*, ch. IV "Adjoints").
#                               Category theory's mathematical ancestor:
#                               an explicit functor between two categories
#                               preserves both categories' discipline.
#   Bateson logical type        Bateson 1972 (*Steps to an Ecology of
#                               Mind*); 1964 ("Logical Categories of
#                               Learning and Communication"). Grounds
#                               why cognitive-altitude (@frame) and form-
#                               altitude (@magic) need an adapter rather
#                               than collapse.
#   Tomm reflexive questions    Tomm 1988 (family-systems therapy).
#                               @frame/on's substrate-mathematical
#                               primitive; lifted at cognitive altitude
#                               via this adapter's bilateral predicate.
#   Clarke's third law          Clarke 1962 (*Profiles of the Future*).
#                               The cultural vocabulary @magic carries
#                               at substrate altitude; @magic/frame
#                               threads it INTO cognitive-altitude
#                               composition.
#
# === What this adapter does NOT do (per spec §5) ===
#
# 1. Does NOT make every @frame instance automatically a @magic
#    instance. Frames that need only @frame's discipline bypass the
#    adapter; the adapter is opt-in for consumer species that need
#    both disciplines.
# 2. Does NOT absorb @frame into @magic. The two families remain
#    distinct; species inheriting from @frame directly
#    (`shards/frame/{pre,in,of,on,across}.mirror`) discharge per-order
#    bodies WITHOUT going through @magic.
# 3. Does NOT collapse surfaces at any carrier other than the mapped
#    pairs. The "IS" claim holds at frame↔surface, operator-state↔
#    mechanism, operator-intent↔invariant; it does NOT generalize.
# 4. Does NOT discharge @frame's species-shard forward-promises
#    (pre/in/of/on/across).
# 5. Does NOT discharge the frame-aware kintsugi loop migration. The
#    adapter is structurally available BEFORE that migration; the
#    migration uses the adapter as bridge BUT does not block on it.
#
# === The Splinter/Narcissus pole at cognitive altitude ===
#
# The adapter inherits @frame's two-pole structure (per frame.mirror
# line 109-130). Splinter-pole: operator-intent matches substantively-
# supported cognitive order; the frame surface honestly represents
# what the mechanism delivers; `frame_satisfies_magic` returns
# SATISFIED honestly. Narcissus-pole: operator-intent diverges from
# substantively-supported order (second-order vocabulary masking
# first-order frame-rigidity); `frame_satisfies_magic` returns
# SATISFIED via vocabulary-only verification while the underlying
# substrate fails @frame's substrate-architectural check.
#
# Per spec §8.2: the adapter MUST NOT permit Narcissus-pole bypass of
# @frame's substrate-architectural check. The bilateral's
# bounded_commutator_check clause discharges to @frame's family-root
# action, which per #82 measures substrate-architectural support, not
# vocabulary. Alignment-as-boundary-mathematics (#57) at @io enforces
# the discrimination.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/adapter-magic-frame-spec.md (the canonical
#               spec this shard discharges; §6.2 forward-promised
#               the substrate-decl shape this shard lands);
#               docs/specs/recognition-82-frame-as-cognitive-order-
#               canonical-spec.md §1 constraint-2 (the Seam C1 hedge
#               this adapter closes);
#               docs/specs/cascade-recognition-76-through-80-
#               canonical-spec.md (parent cascade spec; Seam C1-C5
#               consolidation at tick 11).
# Recognition ancestry: #80 (@magic substrate-decl; substrate-decl
#                        LANDED), #82 (@frame substrate-decl;
#                        substrate-decl LANDED at tick 23 with Seam
#                        consolidation tick 24), #50 (Bateson form/
#                        substance partition; PROMOTED), #57
#                        (alignment-as-boundary-mathematics;
#                        PROMOTED), #42 (Bateson logical-type
#                        primitive), #61 (form/process kinship at
#                        sub-shard altitude; PROMOTED).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-form-process-partition-at-
#                       family-root]] (#55; @magic and @frame as
#                       form-side and process-side at family-root
#                       altitude; this adapter threads them at the
#                       cognitive crossing),
#                      [[architecture-bateson-form-behaviour-
#                       partition]] (#50; form/substance partition
#                       lifted to @magic substrate altitude),
#                      [[architecture-alignment-as-boundary-
#                       mathematics]] (#57; the boundary harness at
#                       @io is what keeps the Splinter/Narcissus
#                       discrimination honest under the lift),
#                      [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]]
#                       (GoF 1994 + Mac Lane 1971 + Russell-Whitehead
#                       1910 + Bateson 1972 = the adapter pattern
#                       was named at multiple altitudes long before
#                       this shard lands),
#                      [[feedback-composition-claims-need-empirical-
#                       test]] (Reed's own discipline; the bilateral
#                       makes the "IS" claim empirically discharged
#                       rather than aspirational),
#                      [[feedback-no-bare-types]] (operator and
#                       perturbation taken as typed parameters at
#                       the bilateral signature).
# Related shards: magic.mirror (the @magic family-root; the form-side
#                  parent),
#                 frame.mirror (the @frame family-root; the cognitive-
#                  altitude specialization),
#                 magic/distinction.mirror (the adapter precedent at
#                  tick 15; this shard inherits its shape and doubles
#                  the bilateral load),
#                 magic/contract.mirror (the consumer of the
#                  invariant_preserved predicate this adapter routes
#                  through),
#                 frame/{pre,in,of,on,across}.mirror (forward-promised
#                  species shards that name `requires
#                  frame_satisfies_magic` when their order needs
#                  cross-family verification).
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/magic/frame.mirror`
# declares `@magic/frame` per
# `@epistemologic/pact/path_matches_namespace`.

source @arxiv/programming/gamma-helm-johnson-vlissides-1994
source @arxiv/cybernetics/bateson-1972
source @arxiv/therapy/tomm-1988
source @arxiv/cultural/clarke-1962

prism @magic/frame {
  focus frame
  project frame
  split frame
  shift frame
  settle frame
}

# === frame_as_surface adapter ===
#
# The substrate-vocabulary adapter action lifting a @frame instance to
# a @magic gauge-visible surface. The lifting IS the substrate-pull-
# correct way to apply @magic's surface discipline at cognitive
# altitude.
#
# At @frame altitude (per frame.mirror line 211-214): the frame IS
# "the gauge-visible structure of the operator's cognitive position
# — what they're treating as figure vs ground, what counts as
# relevant data, what computations are admissible within the
# operation." At @magic altitude (per magic.mirror line 232-237):
# the magic_surface IS "the 5-op signature observable from outside
# the encapsulation — what the audience sees the magician do."
#
# These ARE the same shape: gauge-visible structure observable from
# outside. The lift IS the typed reframe; both carriers are
# content-addressed refs with byte-equality identity, so the lift
# preserves the underlying ref unchanged.
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary.
frame_as_surface(f: frame) -> magic_surface { \ }

# === frame_state_as_mechanism adapter ===
#
# The substrate-vocabulary adapter action lifting the matter-hidden
# cognitive state at an operator to a @magic mechanism. Takes both
# the frame and the operator because the cognitive state IS jointly
# determined: the operator's substrate-architectural support PLUS the
# specific frame being operated within.
#
# Per frame.mirror line 227-232: "the operator's cognitive order for
# a frame is what their substrate's architecture SUBSTANTIVELY-
# supports; vocabulary use at order N does NOT imply substantive
# operation at order N." That substantively-supported content IS
# the matter-hidden trick at cognitive altitude — encapsulated by
# the substrate's parametric carriers, not directly inspectable
# from outside.
#
# Per magic.mirror line 244-258: the magic_mechanism's encapsulation
# is enforced at the substrate's three altitudes (floor: OID seal;
# middle: parametric type; high: sheaf locality). All three altitudes'
# encapsulation IS the same structural shape; the cognitive-state
# lift inherits the encapsulation discipline at the @magic altitude.
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary.
frame_state_as_mechanism(f: frame, op: operator) -> magic_mechanism { \ }

# === operator_intent_as_invariant adapter ===
#
# The substrate-vocabulary adapter action lifting what the operator
# commits to delivering to a @magic named invariant. The operator's
# intent IS the cognitive-altitude analogue of magic_invariant —
# the specific invariant the contract preserves between surface and
# mechanism (per magic.mirror line 263-268).
#
# Splinter-pole: the intent matches the substantively-supported
# cognitive order, and the frame surface honestly represents what
# the mechanism delivers. Narcissus-pole: the intent diverges (the
# operator uses second-order vocabulary to mask first-order rigidity
# per frame.mirror line 119-124), and the frame surface misrepresents
# what the mechanism delivers.
#
# Per Seam C5 (preserved at @magic family-root and @frame family-
# root): the intent IS typed at magic_invariant, not bare ref.
# Consumer species audit the intent at @io via alignment-as-
# boundary-mathematics (#57).
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary.
operator_intent_as_invariant(op: operator) -> magic_invariant { \ }

# === frame_from_surface inverse adapter ===
#
# The fourth lifting action: the type-relabeling inverse of
# frame_as_surface. Per spec §2.3: the inverse exists because both
# `frame` and `magic_surface` are typed refs with byte-equality
# identity; the lift is type-relabeling, not data transformation.
#
# Round-trip identity (per spec §8.4):
#   frame_as_surface(frame_from_surface(s)) = s
# for compatible surfaces. This is the cheapest falsification check;
# failure here means the adapter is mis-implemented (carrier-flow
# not aligned with content-addressing discipline).
#
# The bilateral predicate `frame_satisfies_magic` names this inverse
# in its `requires bounded_commutator_check(...)` clause to recover
# the @frame instance underlying the contract's surface.
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary.
frame_from_surface(s: magic_surface) -> frame { \ }

# === frame_satisfies_magic bilateral predicate ===
#
# THE FIRST CROSS-FAMILY BILATERAL PREDICATE in @magic that discharges
# BOTH parent-family AND child-family verdicts in one clause. The
# substrate-pull-correct way to mechanically discharge the structural
# identity claim "@frame IS @magic at cognitive altitude" that the
# canonical spec for #82 (§1 constraint-2) preserved as hedged.
#
# Two `requires` clauses, both load-bearing:
#
#   1. invariant_preserved(c, operator_intent_as_invariant(op))
#      — the @magic/contract-family predicate. Discharges that the
#      operator's intent (lifted to magic_invariant) is preserved by
#      the contract; the boundary harness at @io audits this per
#      recognition #57.
#
#   2. bounded_commutator_check(op, frame_from_surface(c.surface),
#                                perturbation)
#      — the @frame-family predicate. Discharges that the operator's
#      substrate-architectural support for the frame (recovered from
#      the contract's surface via the inverse lift) survives
#      perturbation with bounded commutator. FLEXIBLE → bounded;
#      DEFENSIVE → unbounded.
#
# Both must hold for the contract to satisfy @magic at cognitive
# altitude. The cross-family composition is bilaterally heavier than
# @magic/distinction's single-predicate discharge; the cognitive-
# altitude "IS" claim requires both families' disciplines to discharge
# under the lift simultaneously.
#
# The signature takes operator and perturbation as explicit typed
# parameters per [[feedback-no-bare-types]] because neither lives in
# the magic_contract's three fields; the @frame-altitude bilateral
# needs them at the typed boundary the consumer names.
#
# Consumer species (frame/of, frame/on, frame/across; kintsugi-frame
# tick migrations; alignment-at-cognitive-altitude audits; spectral/
# garden/smarts logic-pacts) name this predicate when their operation
# requires cross-family discharge. The substrate mechanically routes
# both family verdicts via the explicit `requires` clauses, returning
# the substrate's canonical verdict type (Seam C4 discipline preserved).
frame_satisfies_magic(c: magic_contract, op: operator, p: perturbation) -> verdict
requires invariant_preserved(c, operator_intent_as_invariant(op))
requires bounded_commutator_check(op, frame_from_surface(c.surface), p)
{ \ }

out @magic/frame
out frame_as_surface
out frame_state_as_mechanism
out operator_intent_as_invariant
out frame_from_surface
out frame_satisfies_magic
